## The three current quantities

### 1. FBA flux rerouting is a discrete response divergence

Let \(p\in B_{\mathrm{FBA}}\) encode environmental, bound, knockout, or objective perturbations. Define
\[
F(p)=
\{v\in\mathbb R^R:
Sv=0,\ \ell(p)\le v\le u(p)\}.
\]

To obtain a single-valued response section, replace arbitrary LP-optimum selection by a regularized optimum, for example
\[
v_\beta(p)
=
\arg\max_{v\in F(p)}
\left(c^\top v-\frac{\beta}{2}\|v\|^2\right),
\qquad \beta>0.
\]
Then set
\[
s_{\mathrm{FBA}}(p)=(p,v_\beta(p)),
\qquad
a_{\mathrm{FBA}}(p,v)=v.
\]

With
\[
W=\operatorname{diag}(w_r),\qquad
m(p,0)=I_{\mathrm{ess}}(p),
\]
the FBA statistic is
\[
\boxed{
\Delta^{\mathrm{FBA}}(p\mid 0)
=
\frac{I_{\mathrm{ess}}(p)}
{2N_{\mathrm{FBA}}(p,0)}
\sum_r w_r
\bigl(v_{\beta,r}(p)-v_{\beta,r}(0)\bigr)^2.
}
\]

The manuscript should use its actual existing normalizer in place of \(N_{\mathrm{FBA}}\). The important point is that the essentiality indicator is part of the realization datum, not part of the definition of geometric curvature.

This quantity should no longer be called \(\kappa_V\). A suitable notation is
\[
\Delta_V^{\mathrm{FBA}}
\quad\text{or}\quad
R_V^{\mathrm{FBA}}.
\]

---

### 2. Time-course squared flux change is a temporal response divergence

Let
\[
\gamma:I\to B,\qquad t\mapsto\gamma(t)
\]
be the experimental or inferred time-course. With \(T_1\) as reference,
\[
\boxed{
\Delta_r^{\mathrm{time}}(t\mid T_1)
=
\frac{w_r}{2N_t}
\bigl(v_r(t)-v_r(T_1)\bigr)^2.
}
\]

The aggregate version is
\[
\Delta^{\mathrm{time}}(t\mid T_1)
=
\frac{1}{2N_t}
\sum_r w_r
\bigl(v_r(t)-v_r(T_1)\bigr)^2.
\]

Thus E10/E22 and E24–E27 concern a pullback of \(\Delta_{\mathfrak R}\) along the trajectory \(\gamma\). They do not directly measure sectional curvature.

---

### 3. Geometric \(\kappa\) is a derived local operator

Reserve \(\kappa\) for the geometric quantity. If the existing construction takes a positive part, contracts over normalized bivectors, and uses a Bregman divergence of the smooth surrogate, define it as
\[
\boxed{
\kappa^{\mathrm{geo}}_{\mathfrak R}(x)
=
\sup_{\substack{\xi\wedge\eta\in\Lambda^2T_xB\\
\|\xi\wedge\eta\|=1}}
\left[
\operatorname{Curv}_x
\Delta_{\mathfrak R}(\xi,\eta)
\right]_+ .
}
\]

Here \(\operatorname{Curv}_x\) must be defined explicitly as either:

1. a contraction of the curvature tensor of the GC-connection;
2. an infinitesimal holonomy operator; or
3. a specified second-variation operator for the surrogate divergence.

These are not interchangeable. The manuscript must choose one and state the corresponding type and normalization.

The notation should therefore be:

\[
\kappa^{\mathrm{geo}}
\quad\text{for curvature,}
\]
\[
\Delta^{\mathrm{FBA}}
\quad\text{for knockout rerouting,}
\]
and
\[
\Delta^{\mathrm{time}}
\quad\text{for temporal deviation.}
\]

They share the same realized divergence, but only the first is a curvature contraction.

