## Precise FBA-to-SAVGS mapping

The formal embedding should be stated as follows.

| FBA object | Geometric object |
|---|---|
| Perturbation parameters \(p\) | Point of the base \(B_{\mathrm{FBA}}\) |
| Active LP basis or active constraint set | Stratum of \(B_{\mathrm{FBA}}\) |
| Feasible flux vectors \(F(p)\) | Fiber \(E_p\) |
| Regularized optimum \(v_\beta(p)\) | Response section \(s(p)\) |
| Flux vector \(v_\beta(p)\) | Observable \(a(s(p))\in Y=\mathbb R^R\) |
| Infinitesimal flux response | \(dv_{\beta,p}(\xi)\in\mathbb R^R\) |
| Finite flux rerouting | Secant \(v_\beta(q)-v_\beta(p)\) |
| Biomass \(\mu(p)=c^\top v_\beta(p)\) | Scalar observable on \(B\) |
| GC parallel transport | Must be constructed from FBA sensitivity data; it is not supplied by FBA automatically |

On an active-set stratum, \(v_\beta\) is smooth under the usual regularity assumptions. A tangent perturbation \(\xi\in T_pB\) maps to reaction-flux tangent data by
\[
\xi\longmapsto dv_{\beta,p}(\xi).
\]

The quadratic divergence induces the pullback semimetric
\[
g_p^{\mathrm{FBA}}(\xi,\zeta)
=
\left\langle
dv_{\beta,p}(\xi),
W\,dv_{\beta,p}(\zeta)
\right\rangle.
\]

If degeneracy must be excluded, use
\[
g_p(\xi,\zeta)
=
\langle dv_{\beta,p}(\xi),Wdv_{\beta,p}(\zeta)\rangle
+\lambda\langle\xi,\zeta\rangle_{G},
\qquad \lambda>0.
\]

### Biomass deficit is not itself a metric

Define the normalized biomass deficit relative to wild type by
\[
\delta_\mu(p\mid 0)
=
\frac{[\mu(0)-\mu(p)]_+}{|\mu(0)|+\varepsilon}.
\]

This is a directed deficit or distance-to-reference observable. It is not a metric on \(B\): it is generally asymmetric and cannot distinguish perturbations with equal biomass.

If a genuine base-space distance is required, define, for example,
\[
d_B(p,q)^2
=
\|p-q\|_G^2
+
\lambda_\mu
\left(
\frac{\mu(p)-\mu(q)}{\mu_*}
\right)^2.
\]

The biomass deficit may then be used as a component of \(d_B\), a normalization \(N\), or a mask, but it should not silently be called “the distance.”

