## What is needed for a genuine curvature theorem

A curvature contraction depends on two independent tangent directions and, for connection curvature, on transport around a loop. A single wild-type/knockout comparison supplies only one secant.

To estimate geometric curvature, the FBA design must contain at least the four perturbation conditions
\[
p,\qquad
p+h\xi,\qquad
p+k\eta,\qquad
p+h\xi+k\eta.
\]

If \(P_{h,k}^{\xi,\eta}\) denotes transport around the corresponding perturbation plaquette, then
\[
\frac{P_{h,k}^{\xi,\eta}-I}{hk}
\longrightarrow
R_p(\xi,\eta)
\]
as \(h,k\to0\), under the usual smoothness assumptions.

A valid discrete curvature estimator would consequently have the form
\[
\widehat\kappa_{h,k}^{\mathrm{FBA}}(p)
=
\sup_{\|\xi\wedge\eta\|=1}
\left[
\mathcal C\!\left(
\frac{P_{h,k}^{\xi,\eta}-I}{hk}
\right)
\right]_+,
\]
with the contraction \(\mathcal C\) matching the definition of
\(\kappa^{\mathrm{geo}}\).

This yields the desired bridge theorem:

> **Discrete realization theorem.**  
> On a fixed smooth active-set stratum, assuming the FBA sensitivity transport realizes the GC-connection and the mask is locally constant,
> \[
> \widehat\kappa_{h,k}^{\mathrm{FBA}}(p)
> \longrightarrow
> \kappa^{\mathrm{geo}}(p)
> \]
> as \(h,k\to0\).

Without the transport construction and paired perturbations, this theorem cannot be claimed.

A one-dimensional time-course likewise cannot identify sectional curvature by itself. It validates response divergence along a trajectory, not curvature over a two-plane.

